# Testing — Go pack

What to test and the four tiers: `testing.mdc`. This file is the Go tooling.

- Runner: `go test -race -count=1 ./...` (`pnpm go:test`); `-race` needs cgo, so a runner needs a C compiler. `foo_test.go` beside `foo.go`, in the `_test` package, so a test only touches what a caller can.
- Table tests (`[]struct{ name … }` + `t.Run`) for examples; property tests with `pgregory.net/rapid`. Worked example: `internal/domain/note_test.go`. rapid prints a failing run's seed (replay with `-rapid.seed=<n>`) and saves it under `testdata/rapid/`: commit that file, it is the regression test.
- Fakes of ports live in `internal/testsupport/`; assert the outcome (what the fake holds), not call counts.
- **Fault injection (tier 2) seed:** `internal/app/notesync/service_chaos_test.go`, one test per fault kind plus a rapid property over random fault scripts. The model server and the fault-scripted outbox are `internal/testsupport/chaos/`. Virtual time is `testing/synctest`: inside `synctest.Test`, `time.Sleep`, timers, and `context.WithTimeout` run on a fake clock that jumps when every goroutine is blocked, so a 10-minute delay costs nothing. rapid reads the test deadline, which a bubble forbids, so rapid sits outside and each run opens its own bubble.
- Don't pipe `go test` through `head`/`grep`: the pipe hides the exit code (`testing.mdc`).
